WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.623
Hi, I'm Gossamer!

00:00:02.623 --> 00:00:06.277
Muse Spark Open is Meta's new open model, and Meta says it

00:00:06.277 --> 00:00:07.800
runs on two Mac Studios.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.845
Here is how something that big fits on a desk.

00:00:12.183 --> 00:00:15.370
The idea: almost every parameter is an expert that sleeps.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:19.832
Each token wakes only eight of them, and they work in a narrower space.

00:00:21.758 --> 00:00:23.524
A token arrives at a layer.

00:00:23.524 --> 00:00:25.880
A router scores every expert and picks eight.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.451
First the token is squeezed to half its width.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:32.167
Only the chosen experts run, in that narrow space.

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:35.723
The rest stay asleep, so each one costs half what it would.

00:00:36.143 --> 00:00:39.435
Then the result is projected back to full width and gated

00:00:39.435 --> 00:00:40.632
into the residual stream.

00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:45.032
Memory for the context is the other problem.

00:00:45.032 --> 00:00:49.229
If every layer remembered every token, a million tokens would need over

00:00:49.229 --> 00:00:50.979
two hundred and sixty gigabytes.

00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:55.459
Instead, three of every four layers only look back a short window.

00:00:55.459 --> 00:01:00.196
Just the global layers keep everything, so the whole cache is about sixty-nine gigabytes.

00:01:01.508 --> 00:01:02.409
Now the bytes.

00:01:02.409 --> 00:01:03.609
Full precision never fits.

00:01:03.609 --> 00:01:06.912
The four-bit build fits two Mac Studios with the whole cache

00:01:06.912 --> 00:01:08.112
and room to spare.

00:01:08.112 --> 00:01:09.914
That is what the claim means.

00:01:11.758 --> 00:01:13.917
Unsloth's two-bit files fit a single Mac.

00:01:13.917 --> 00:01:17.619
Their own chart shows the price: the smaller the file, the further

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:19.470
it drifts from the full model.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.738
The two-bit file that fits the smaller Mac drifts more than the

00:01:23.738 --> 00:01:25.342
one Unsloth recommends for agents.

00:01:27.183 --> 00:01:28.891
So the trick is not magic.

00:01:28.891 --> 00:01:32.023
Sleeping experts and forgetful layers are what put a model this

00:01:32.023 --> 00:01:33.162
size on a desk.

00:01:34.733 --> 00:01:36.039
Eight experts per token.

00:01:36.039 --> 00:01:37.671
Short memory in most layers.

00:01:37.671 --> 00:01:39.302
Four bits on two Macs.

00:01:39.722 --> 00:01:41.919
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:41.919 --> 00:01:42.547
I'm Gossamer.

00:01:42.547 --> 00:01:42.861
Bye!
